    {
      "fullUrl": "urn:uuid:b903eaea-6ccb-4568-b41c-603cefa5eba3",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "ServiceRequest",
        "id": "svcreq-0",
        "status": "draft",
        "intent": "proposal",
        "priority": "urgent",
        "code": {
          "coding": [
            {
              "display": "GLA gene sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis"
            }
          ],
          "text": "GLA gene sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "urn:uuid:1261f64e-00bf-468b-9502-bfbfd594ee3a"
        },
        "reasonCode": [
          {
            "text": "The patient's phenotype (renal failure, proteinuria, cardiomyopathy, pain, anemia, electrolyte abnormalities) and family history (similar symptoms in daughter and maternal cousin) are highly suggestive of Fabry disease. The top candidate gene identified is GLA, which encodes the enzyme alpha-galactosidase A. Sequencing the GLA gene will identify pathogenic variants, while deletion/duplication analysis will detect larger rearrangements that sequencing might miss."
          }
        ],
        "note": [
          {
            "text": "Power: decisive. A positive result would establish: Fabry disease if a pathogenic variant in GLA is identified.. A negative result would rule out: Fabry disease if no pathogenic variant in GLA is found (though deep intronic or regulatory variants might be missed).."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "urn:uuid:275bb28d-fa8b-4d55-8dcf-541f922b55fd",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "ServiceRequest",
        "id": "svcreq-1",
        "status": "draft",
        "intent": "proposal",
        "priority": "urgent",
        "code": {
          "coding": [
            {
              "display": "Alpha-galactosidase A enzyme activity assay"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Alpha-galactosidase A enzyme activity assay"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "urn:uuid:1261f64e-00bf-468b-9502-bfbfd594ee3a"
        },
        "reasonCode": [
          {
            "text": "Fabry disease is caused by deficient alpha-galactosidase A activity. Measuring this enzyme activity in leukocytes or plasma can directly assess the underlying biochemical defect. This test complements genetic testing."
          }
        ],
        "note": [
          {
            "text": "Power: decisive. A positive result would establish: Fabry disease if alpha-galactosidase A activity is significantly reduced or absent.. A negative result would rule out: Fabry disease if alpha-galactosidase A activity is normal or near normal (though some variants may have residual activity).."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
